"# Chapter 4 — The Standards Boundary

Scheme is not one language; it is a family of standards that have argued with each other for decades. R5RS, R6RS, R7RS, and a long shelf of SRFIs each stake out a version of the language, and they do not agree on everything. Some disagree loudly — about modules, about hash tables, about the very argument order of a fold. When you write Motoi, you are not writing \"Scheme in general.\" You are writing one particular, deliberate selection *from* that family, and the strays that trip up careful people almost always come from a *neighboring* standard rather than from some wholly foreign language. This chapter draws the standards boundary sharply, so that when a verb feels like Scheme but doesn't work, you can name which standard it wandered in from.

The through-line of the book applies here with full force: the edge is knowledge, not embarrassment. Knowing that `fold-left` is R6RS and `foldl` is Racket is not trivia. It is the exact knowledge that lets you reach past the wrong fold and grab the right one without hesitation.

## The lineage in brief

A quick map of the family, because the names matter.

**R5RS** (1998) is the classic small Scheme — the one many people first learned. It is elegant and minimal. It has no module system to speak of, no hash-table standard, and its list library is thin. Much of what R5RS lacked, later work supplied. R5RS is still the ancestor of every Scheme dialect, and a great deal of ordinary Scheme code — `define`, `lambda`, `let`, `cond`, `cons`, `map`, `for-each` — comes directly from R5RS unchanged. When you use the core of Motoi, you are using code whose lineage runs straight back to R5RS.

**R6RS** (2007) is the large, ambitious Scheme. It brought a full module system (`library`, `import`), a hash-table library (`make-hashtable`, `hashtable-ref`), records, exceptions, and — crucially for us — `fold-left` and `fold-right` with an *accumulator-first* procedure convention. R6RS is powerful and heavy, and its choices are the ones most likely to feel like home to a programmer who then trips on Motoi.

**R7RS-small** (2013) is a deliberate return to a small core, closer in spirit to R5RS but modernized. It defines `import` and `define-library` for its own module story, keeps the core lean, and leaves the richer libraries to the SRFIs. **This is Motoi's authority** — the semantic bedrock from Chapter 2.

**The SRFIs** are the à-la-carte library requests — numbered, each self-contained. SRFI-1 (lists), SRFI-13 (strings), SRFI-125 (hash tables), SRFI-27 (random) are the four Motoi adopts. Each is its own part with its own spec, and Motoi picks these four *specifically*, not the whole SRFI shelf.

**Racket** is not a Scheme standard at all but a large descendant language with its own dialect — `require`, `foldl`, `for/list`, `mapcar`-like variants. It is the source of a distinct set of strays.

## Where Motoi sits

Motoi's position on this map is precise and worth stating flatly: **R7RS-small is the core and the authority; SRFI-1, SRFI-13, SRFI-125, and SRFI-27 are bundled as named parts; R6RS and Racket are *not* adopted, and neither is the R7RS module ceremony as a user-facing thing.** Motoi keeps R7RS-small's *semantics* while dropping its *import ritual* — the libraries auto-load, as Chapter 2 showed. So Motoi is, in one sentence, R7RS-small semantics + four chosen SRFIs + Motoi's own libraries, with no import and no module forms exposed to you.

Everything runnable in this book lives in exactly that region. Here is a program that is pure Motoi-standard — R7RS-small core plus the four SRFIs — with not a single verb from R6RS or Racket:

```scheme
;; Pure Motoi standard: R7RS-small core + SRFI-1/13/125/27, nothing else.
(define (summarize xs)
  (list (fold + 0 xs)               ; SRFI-1
        (sort xs <)                  ; core-standard sort
        (delete-duplicates xs)))     ; SRFI-1
(summarize (list 3 1 2 3 1))
```

## The canonical contrast: three folds

The clearest place the standards boundary shows itself is the fold. Three standards define a left fold, and they do not agree — not on the name, and not on the argument order the procedure receives. This is *the* worked contrast to hold in your head, because it stands for the whole boundary.

**SRFI-1 `fold`** — Motoi's fold. Called `(fold proc init list)`, and the procedure receives `(elem acc)`: the element first, the running accumulator second. This is yours, and it runs:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 fold — Motoi's. Procedure gets (elem acc): element first.
(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

The element-first convention is not arbitrary; it makes `cons` fall out naturally as \"prepend,\" which is why folding `cons` over a list reverses it — a fact you can lean on:

```scheme
;; Element-first means (fold cons '() ...) reverses. SRFI-1 contract.
(fold cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

**R6RS `fold-left`** — *not* Motoi. Called `(fold-left proc acc list)`, and the procedure receives `(acc elem)`: the accumulator first, the element second. The opposite convention. Motoi deliberately excludes this name. If your fingers type `fold-left`, you have crossed into R6RS, and Motoi will not bind it. It lives in this book only in prose, never in a runnable fence — because it is not a verb you have. The correction is always the same: reach for SRFI-1 `fold` and remember the argument order flips.

**Racket `foldl`** — also *not* Motoi. Racket spells it `foldl`, and its procedure-argument convention differs again. `foldl` is Racket's; it is not in Motoi and does not appear in any runnable code here. The correction, once more, is `fold`.

So three names — `fold`, `fold-left`, `foldl` — for the same shape of operation, and Motoi has committed to exactly one of them. When you see either of the other two, you can name its home instantly: `fold-left` is R6RS, `foldl` is Racket, and neither is yours. The *right* verb, `fold`, is always waiting, and its companion `fold-right` handles the right-associated case, SRFI-1 style, element-first:

```scheme
;; The right-associated partner is fold-right — also SRFI-1, element-first.
(fold-right cons '() (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

### Why the fold order matters in practice

For symmetric operations like `+`, the order of `(elem acc)` vs `(acc elem)` makes no observable difference — `(+ x y)` equals `(+ y x)`. But the moment you use a non-symmetric operation, the order is everything. Building a description string by prepending each element shows the difference clearly: with element-first, the element arrives first in the body of the lambda and is prepended; reverse the order and you get a different output.

```scheme
;; fold builds a list in reverse (element-first means prepend).
;; This is the SRFI-1 contract; know it cold.
(fold (lambda (elem acc) (cons (* elem elem) acc))
      '()
      (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

And `fold-right` to preserve order:

```scheme
;; fold-right builds in the original order (processes right-to-left).
(fold-right (lambda (elem acc) (cons (* elem elem) acc))
            '()
            (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

The two results are the square-numbers list in different orders — reversed for `fold`, preserved for `fold-right`. Neither is wrong; they are different operations. The point is that you must know which one you have, and Motoi has given you exactly the SRFI-1 signatures for both.

## Hash tables: another standards fork

The fold is the famous case, but the same fork runs through hash tables. **R6RS** spells them `make-hashtable`, `hashtable-set!`, `hashtable-ref` — no hyphen between \"hash\" and \"table.\" **SRFI-125**, which Motoi adopts, spells them `hash-table`, `hash-table-set!`, `hash-table-ref` — hyphenated — plus the friendlier `hash-table-ref/default`. The R6RS names are strays; the SRFI-125 names are yours. Watch the real ones work:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125 hash tables — hyphenated names — are Motoi's. Not R6RS hashtable-*.
(define t (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! t \"pi\" 3)
(hash-table-set! t \"e\" 2)
(list (hash-table-ref/default t \"pi\" 0)
      (hash-table-contains? t \"e\")
      (hash-table-size t))
```

If you remember `hashtable-ref` from an R6RS tutorial, that memory is pointing at the wrong standard. The reference (Chapter 3) will correct you to the hyphenated SRFI-125 name every time.

### The R6RS hash-table walkthrough

Let's be specific about what R6RS provides and what Motoi provides, side by side in prose, so the difference is unmistakable:

| Operation | R6RS spelling | Motoi/SRFI-125 spelling |
|-----------|--------------|------------------------|
| Create | `(make-hashtable hash-fn equiv-fn)` | `(hash-table)` |
| Insert | `(hashtable-set! ht k v)` | `(hash-table-set! ht k v)` |
| Look up (error on miss) | `(hashtable-ref ht k default)` | `(hash-table-ref ht k thunk)` |
| Look up (default on miss) | `(hashtable-ref ht k default)` | `(hash-table-ref/default ht k default)` |
| Contains? | `(hashtable-contains? ht k)` | `(hash-table-contains? ht k)` |
| Size | `(hashtable-size ht)` | `(hash-table-size ht)` |

The differences are small — a hyphen here, an argument convention there — but they are real and they cause real crashes. The Motoi column is always the SRFI-125 column.

## Modules: the ceremony Motoi drops

R6RS gives you `library` and `import`; R7RS-small gives you `define-library` and `import`; Racket gives you `require` and `module`. All three are ways to *package and pull in* code. Motoi drops the user-facing ceremony entirely — the libraries auto-load, so there is no `import`, no `library`, no `define-library`, no `require`, no `module` you ever write. This is the single largest divergence from every standard on the shelf, and it is a *positive* choice, not an omission: in a small teaching dialect, always-on batteries beat a namespace ritual you can forget to perform.

The practical consequence is that any line beginning with one of those words is, in Motoi, a stray — and you can name its standard: `(import ...)` and `(define-library ...)` are R7RS/R6RS ceremony, `(require ...)` and `(module ...)` are Racket. None appear in runnable Motoi. What *does* appear is code that simply uses a library verb with no preamble, because that is the whole point:

```scheme
;; No import, no module. The SRFI-13 verb is just here, ready.
(string-join (map string-upcase (list \"no\" \"import\" \"needed\")) \" \")
```

## Other standards-boundary cases beyond the fold

The fold and hash-table families are the largest and most commonly encountered fork cases, but the standards boundary runs through a few other areas too.

**List predicates:** R6RS defines `for-all` and `exists` as the names for universal and existential quantification over a list. SRFI-1, which Motoi adopts, calls them `every` and `any`. If code contains `for-all` or `exists`, those are R6RS names — the SRFI-1 replacements run correctly:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 every and any — not R6RS for-all / exists.
(list (every even? (list 2 4 6))
      (any odd? (list 2 4 5)))
```

**String comparisons:** R7RS-small provides both case-sensitive (`string<?`, `string=?`) and case-insensitive (`string-ci<?`, `string-ci=?`) comparators. These are core, and they're present without importing anything. R6RS defined the same names, so there's no conflict here — but worth knowing that both standards agree on the string-comparison family:

```scheme
;; Core string comparison: case-sensitive and case-insensitive.
(list (string<? \"abc\" \"abd\")
      (string-ci=? \"Hello\" \"hello\"))
```

**Exact/inexact arithmetic:** R7RS-small carries `exact->inexact` and `inexact->exact` for numeric tower conversions. R6RS renamed these to `exact` and `inexact`. Motoi's names are the R7RS-small ones: `exact->inexact` and `inexact->exact`. If code uses bare `exact` or `inexact` as a conversion verb, those are the R6RS names:

```scheme
;; R7RS-small names: exact->inexact and inexact->exact.
(list (exact->inexact 1/3)
      (inexact->exact 0.5))
```

## Why exclude perfectly good standard verbs on purpose?

It can feel strange that Motoi *refuses* real, working Scheme verbs. Why not accept `fold-left` too, as a convenience for R6RS refugees? The answer is that a language which accepts *both* argument-order conventions teaches its users *neither*. If both `fold` and `fold-left` bind, then every reader of every fold must first work out which convention this particular call assumed — is the procedure getting `(elem acc)` or `(acc elem)`? That ambiguity is a tax levied on every future line, forever. By binding exactly one fold, Motoi makes the answer automatic: it is always element-first, always SRFI-1, and the accumulator-first shape is always a signal that you are looking at foreign code.

The exclusion is therefore a *teaching device*, not a limitation. It converts a silent, per-call ambiguity into a loud, once-and-for-all boundary. The same logic explains the single hash-table spelling, the single set of list verbs, the absence of module ceremony. Motoi would rather be small and unambiguous than large and blurry. Every excluded standard name is a place where the language chose clarity over breadth — and, not incidentally, a place where a stray becomes easy to recognize precisely *because* only one right answer exists.

## The standards boundary as a placement tool

Let's use the standards boundary as a fast classification tool, the way Chapter 1 used the three-region framework. When a Scheme-flavored verb fails in Motoi, the first question is: which standard is it from?

- `fold-left` → R6RS. Correct to SRFI-1 `fold`, remember to flip argument order.
- `foldl` → Racket. Same correction.
- `foldr` → Racket/Haskell. Correct to SRFI-1 `fold-right`.
- `for-all` → R6RS. Correct to SRFI-1 `every`.
- `exists` → R6RS. Correct to SRFI-1 `any`.
- `make-hashtable` → R6RS. Correct to SRFI-125 `hash-table`.
- `hashtable-ref` → R6RS. Correct to SRFI-125 `hash-table-ref/default`.
- `import` → R7RS-small ceremony (or R6RS). No analog — drop the line.
- `require` → Racket. No analog — drop the line.
- `exact` (as conversion) → R6RS name. Use `inexact->exact` (R7RS-small).
- `inexact` (as conversion) → R6RS name. Use `exact->inexact` (R7RS-small).

And the analogs for those that have them, running together:

```scheme
;; The analogs for the strays above, all real, all running together.
(let ((table (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! table \"a\" 1)
  (list (fold + 0 (list 2 4 6))                        ; not fold-left
        (fold-right cons '() (list 1 2 3))             ; not foldr
        (hash-table-ref/default table \"a\" 0)          ; not hashtable-ref
        (every even? (list 2 4 6))                     ; not for-all
        (any odd? (list 2 3 4))                        ; not exists
        (exact->inexact 1/4)))                         ; not (inexact ...)
```

## Placing a stray by its standard

Put the boundary to work. Given a Scheme-flavored verb that failed, name the standard it came from and the Motoi analog:

- `fold-left` — R6RS (accumulator-first). Analog: SRFI-1 `fold`, remembering the order flips.
- `foldl` / `foldr` — Racket. Analog: SRFI-1 `fold` / `fold-right`.
- `hashtable-ref`, `make-hashtable` — R6RS. Analog: SRFI-125 `hash-table-ref/default`, `hash-table`.
- `import`, `define-library`, `library` — R7RS/R6RS module ceremony. Analog: nothing — libraries auto-load.
- `require`, `module` — Racket packaging. Analog: nothing — libraries auto-load.
- `for/list` — Racket comprehension. Analog: `map` over `filter`.

The standards boundary is the sharpest edge in the whole language, because the strays here are the most convincing — they are *Scheme*, from Scheme's own family, and they carry the authority of a published standard. That is exactly why they are worth learning cold. A verb from Python or C announces its foreignness; a verb from R6RS whispers that it belongs. Learn the fold contrast, the hash-table spelling, the module drop, the list-predicate rename, and you will never again mistake a neighboring standard's verb for one of yours.

## The SRFI shelf: adopted versus available

One nuance worth stating clearly: Motoi adopts *four* SRFIs from a shelf of hundreds. The four — SRFI-1, SRFI-13, SRFI-125, SRFI-27 — are chosen because together they cover the everyday work of lists, strings, associative lookup, and randomness without overlap and without redundancy. The other SRFIs are not adopted, which means their verbs are not present, even though those SRFIs are real, numbered, published specifications.

SRFI-1 is the list library — chosen. SRFI-6 is a string port library — not adopted. SRFI-8 provides `receive` for multiple-value returns — not adopted; Motoi uses `call-with-values` or `let-values`. SRFI-17 is a generalized `set!` — not adopted. SRFI-43 is a vector library — not adopted; Motoi uses the R7RS-small vector verbs. SRFI-69 is an older hash-table library — not adopted; Motoi uses SRFI-125.

The practical consequence: when you encounter a verb from an unadopted SRFI, it is a stray — but a stray from a real, numbered standard. Placing it correctly means naming both the SRFI and the fact that Motoi did not adopt that SRFI. The correction is either a Motoi analog or an honest null if the functionality is truly absent.

SRFI-43's `vector-map` and `vector-for-each` are examples. They are real SRFI verbs, but SRFI-43 is not adopted. For basic vector iteration, Motoi uses `vector->list` plus `map`, which is equivalent and uses only adopted verbs:

```scheme
;; No SRFI-43 vector-map. Use vector->list + map + list->vector if needed.
(list->vector (map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (vector->list (vector 1 2 3 4))))
```

## The R5RS inheritance

R5RS, as the ancestor standard, contributes most of the core vocabulary that remains unchanged across all later Scheme standards. The verbs from R5RS that matter in everyday use are uncontroversially yours in Motoi: `define`, `lambda`, `let`, `cond`, `case`, `and`, `or`, `not`, `if`, `begin`, `set!`, `cons`, `car`, `cdr`, `list`, `pair?`, `null?`, `append`, `reverse`, `map`, `for-each`, `apply`, `eval`, `not`, `equal?`, `eqv?`, `eq?`, the numeric tower, and the string and character operations.

R5RS's list and string primitives form the floor that SRFI-1 and SRFI-13 extend. For instance, R5RS defines `map`, `for-each`, `assoc`, `member`, `string-length`, `string-ref` — all present in Motoi unchanged. SRFI-1 extends `map` to multiple lists and adds `fold`, `filter`, `iota`, and dozens more. SRFI-13 extends the string vocabulary with `string-upcase`, `string-join`, `string-contains`, and its own wide shelf.

The key point is that R5RS strays are rare: almost everything R5RS defined is still in Motoi unchanged. The boundary problems come from R6RS and Racket, not R5RS. If a verb feels old and core and Lispy, it is probably R5RS, and it is probably yours:

```scheme
;; R5RS core verbs: all present in Motoi, unchanged since 1998.
(let ((xs (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
  (list (apply + xs)
        (reverse xs)
        (assoc 3 (list (list 1 \"a\") (list 3 \"c\") (list 5 \"e\")))
        (member 3 xs)))
```

## Why R7RS-small, not R6RS?

The question sometimes arises: if R6RS is more capable, why did Motoi choose R7RS-small as its authority? The answer is at the level of philosophy, and it matters for understanding why the boundary is where it is.

R6RS was designed by committee to be comprehensive: a full Scheme that could compete with Common Lisp in industrial settings, with records, exceptions, libraries, hash tables, a rich I/O system, and carefully specified behavior for edge cases. It is a serious, capable language. But comprehensiveness has a cost: R6RS is large, and learning where all the pieces fit takes time. The module system alone has several forms. The hash-table library has its own set of equality predicates and hash functions to manage.

R7RS-small is a deliberate retreat to smallness. The committee that designed it looked at R6RS, noted that many Scheme implementors did not implement it fully, and asked: what is the smallest language that covers the common needs of Scheme programmers? R7RS-small's answer is: a clean core plus a clear door to the SRFIs for the rest. You learn the core, you pick the SRFIs you need, and the language stays navigable.

Motoi's choice of R7RS-small is therefore a choice of navigability over comprehensiveness. The four SRFIs fill in the most common needs — lists, strings, hash tables, randomness — without the weight of a full library system. The result is a language you can hold in your head, which is exactly what a teaching dialect should be.

## Seven more standards-boundary placements

Let's drill the classification skill across a wider sample:

- `string-copy!` — exists in R7RS-small (fills into an existing string). Motoi: may or may not wire this specific form; check the reference. Core string mutation is `string-set!`.
- `bytevector` — R7RS-small adds bytevectors for binary data. In Motoi, check the reference; the core bytevector operations may be present.
- `receive` — SRFI-8, for capturing multiple values. Not adopted. Analog: `call-with-values` or `let-values`.
- `srfi-1:list-copy` — SRFI-1's `list-copy` is adopted. Yours.
- `assert` — R6RS's `assert` from the conditions library. Not in Motoi. Analog: a manual `if` + error.
- `environment` — R7RS-small's first-class environment. In Motoi, check the reference.
- `string-for-each` — R7RS-small. Likely present; applies a procedure to each character:

```scheme
;; string-for-each: R7RS-small, applies proc to each character.
(let ((chars '()))
  (string-for-each (lambda (c) (set! chars (cons c chars))) \"motoi\")
  (reverse chars))
```

The classification habit is the same regardless of the verb: which standard family does it come from, is that family adopted, and if not, what is the Motoi analog?

## Dialogues at the standards boundary

---

*(informative, fold argument order)*

> \"I keep getting unexpected results from my fold. I'm passing `(acc elem)` to the procedure.\"

That's the R6RS `fold-left` convention. Motoi uses SRFI-1 `fold`, which passes `(elem acc)` — element first, accumulator second. So the correction is to swap the lambda's argument names, not the verb:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 fold: lambda gets (elem acc). Swap from the R6RS (acc elem) order.
(fold (lambda (elem acc) (+ acc (* elem 2))) 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

---

*(brief, hash-table spelling)*

> \"Why is `hashtable-ref` unbound?\"

R6RS spelling. Motoi uses SRFI-125: `hash-table-ref/default` with a hyphen.

---

*(friendly, module ceremony)*

> \"Do I need to `(import (scheme list))` before using SRFI-1?\"

Nope — everything is already loaded. Just use the verbs. `fold`, `filter`, `iota` are all right here:

```scheme
;; No import needed. SRFI-1 is the battery; it's already on.
(iota 5)
```

---

## Cross-dialect comparisons for each major boundary case

Working through concrete cross-dialect comparisons makes the boundary feel real rather than abstract. Here are the three main conflict zones worked out across all the relevant dialects:

**Zone 1: the fold.** Motoi's SRFI-1 `fold` is element-first. Here is the same summation expressed in the argument order of each dialect, in prose: in R6RS, `(fold-left + 0 (list 1 2 3))` where `+` receives `(acc elem)` — accumulator first. In Racket, `(foldl + 0 (list 1 2 3))` where `+` receives `(elem acc)` — same as SRFI-1 in this case, but the name is different and the behavior can diverge for non-symmetric operations. In Haskell, `foldl (+) 0 [1,2,3]` where the lambda receives `(acc elem)` — R6RS order in a functional-syntax notation. Only one of those — the SRFI-1 `fold` — runs in Motoi:

```scheme
;; The one fold that runs: SRFI-1 (elem acc). All others are strays.
(fold (lambda (elem acc) (cons elem acc)) '() (list 1 2 3))
```

**Zone 2: hash tables.** The creation, insertion, and lookup all have dialect-specific spellings. In R6RS: `make-hashtable`, `hashtable-set!`, `hashtable-ref`. In SRFI-69 (an older SRFI Motoi does not adopt): `make-hash-table`, `hash-table-set!`, `hash-table-ref`. In SRFI-125 (what Motoi adopts): `hash-table`, `hash-table-set!`, `hash-table-ref/default`. The names overlap partially, which makes casual reading confusing. In runnable Motoi:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125: the spelling that works. Hyphenated hash-table, no make-.
(let ((t (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! t \"answer\" 42)
  (hash-table-ref/default t \"answer\" 0))
```

**Zone 3: module ceremony.** In R6RS: `(library (my lib) (export ...) (import ...) ...)`. In R7RS-small: `(define-library (my lib) (export ...) (import ...) ...)`. In Racket: `(module my-lib racket ...)` and `(require my-lib)`. In Motoi: nothing — no library form, no import, no module. The absence is the whole category. A program in Motoi goes:

```scheme
;; Motoi has no module ceremony. First line is code, not packaging.
(define (greet name)
  (string-append \"Hello, \" name \"!\"))
(greet \"world\")
```

## The boundary in narrative: a standards-confusion story

Someone arrives at Motoi carrying months of R6RS practice. Their muscle memory produces `fold-left` naturally, because that's what they've typed hundreds of times. They write a little program:

```scheme
;; This is the Motoi version of what they meant to write.
;; In their head they typed fold-left but we show the corrected fold.
(let ((nums (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
  (fold (lambda (x acc) (cons (* x x) acc))
        '()
        nums))
```

When they type `fold-left` instead (which we show only in prose — not in a runnable fence), the interpreter responds with an unbound symbol. The programmer is confused: this is Scheme, this is standard, why is it unbound? The answer is: you are right that it is standard — it is R6RS — and Motoi is R7RS-small + SRFI-1, and those standards made a different choice. The verb that covers your intent here is `fold`, and the argument order your lambda receives is `(elem acc)` rather than `(acc elem)`.

Once they know the boundary, the correction takes five seconds and the program runs. But the confusion before knowing the boundary can cost much more. This is why the chapter exists: not to embarrass anyone for not knowing R6RS vs SRFI-1, but to give everyone the map they need to find the right verb in seconds.

A different confusing case: the R6RS hash-table family. Someone writes `(make-hashtable equal-hash equal?)` as R6RS specifies. The interpreter says `equal-hash` is unbound — because that's an R6RS hash-function name, and Motoi's SRFI-125 hash table simply takes no explicit hash function (it uses `equal?` by default when you call `(hash-table)`). Again, the correction is fast once you have the map:

```scheme
;; The corrected SRFI-125 call: no hash function argument needed.
(let ((h (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! h (list 1 2) \"pair-key\")
  (hash-table-ref/default h (list 1 2) \"not found\"))
```

Two confusions, both from R6RS, both corrected in seconds with the standards map.

## The boundary as an identity marker

There is a deeper point beneath the classification exercise. When Motoi says \"I'm not R6RS\" or \"fold-left is not one of my verbs,\" that is not just error correction. It is *self-knowledge*. A language that knows its own standards boundary knows what it is, not just what it lacks. Motoi is R7RS-small + four SRFIs. That is a positive, complete description. Every exclusion of a neighboring standard reinforces that description: by saying \"fold-left is R6RS and not mine,\" Motoi is simultaneously saying \"fold (SRFI-1) is mine, element-first, always.\"

The standards boundary is therefore a two-sided thing. On one side: the excluded verbs from neighboring standards, each named and filed. On the other: the included verbs from the chosen standards, each confirmed and running. Every time you correctly name a stray as R6RS or Racket, you simultaneously confirm the positive inventory that Chapter 2 built. The two exercises are the same exercise from different starting points.

This is why the through-line of the book uses the phrase \"the edge of your language is knowledge, not embarrassment.\" Knowing that `fold-left` is not yours is not a gap; it is an exact piece of self-description. The gap would be not knowing. When you can say fluently \"that's R6RS, and my fold is SRFI-1's fold, element-first,\" you have said something precise about what Motoi is. That precision is identity.

## The memory a standards-aware programmer carries

A programmer who has internalized the standards boundary carries a small set of key contrasts — not hundreds of facts, but the load-bearing ones that explain most of the confusion:

1. **Fold:** one fold in Motoi (SRFI-1, element-first). `fold-left` is R6RS (acc-first). `foldl` is Racket.
2. **Hash tables:** hyphenated names in Motoi (SRFI-125). No-hyphen names are R6RS.
3. **Modules:** no module forms in Motoi. Any line with `import`, `require`, `library`, or `module` is a stray.
4. **List predicates:** `every` / `any` (SRFI-1). R6RS says `for-all` / `exists`.
5. **Numeric conversion:** `exact->inexact` (R7RS-small). R6RS renamed to `inexact`.

Five facts. That is the whole standards boundary compressed to its most useful form. Everything else is derivable from \"Motoi is R7RS-small + SRFI-1/13/125/27, excluding R6RS conventions and Racket.\"

Here is a program that uses four of those five distinctions correctly — the ones that have runnable analogs:

```scheme
;; All four runnable standards-boundary distinctions in one program.
(let ((h (hash-table))           ; SRFI-125, not R6RS make-hashtable
      (xs (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
  (hash-table-set! h \"sum\" (fold + 0 xs))          ; SRFI-1 fold, not fold-left
  (hash-table-set! h \"all-pos\" (every positive? xs)) ; SRFI-1 every, not for-all
  (hash-table-set! h \"as-real\" (exact->inexact 1/5)) ; R7RS-small, not inexact
  (list (hash-table-ref/default h \"sum\" 0)
        (hash-table-ref/default h \"all-pos\" #f)
        (hash-table-ref/default h \"as-real\" 0.0)))
```

Five correct choices, one program, zero standards mistakes. That is what carrying the standards boundary looks like in practice.

## Extending the probe habit to standards verification

The reference-and-interpreter pipeline from Chapter 3 applies here with a specific flavor: when you encounter a Scheme verb you are uncertain about, the first question is not just \"does it exist?\" but \"which standard defined it, and did Motoi adopt that standard?\"

A quick probe habit for standards questions:

1. Write the verb in a minimal expression.
2. If the interpreter says `unbound symbol`, the verb is not in Motoi's battery.
3. Check whether the verb name is a known R6RS or Racket name.
4. If yes, you have identified the standard and can reach for the Motoi analog.
5. Probe the analog to confirm it works.

Applied to `for-all`:

- Try `(for-all even? (list 2 4 6))` — unbound.
- Recognize `for-all` as an R6RS name for universal quantification.
- Reach for SRFI-1 `every`.
- Probe:

```scheme
;; every: the SRFI-1 replacement for R6RS for-all. Confirmed here.
(every even? (list 2 4 6))
```

Applied to `make-hashtable`:

- Try `(make-hashtable ...)` — unbound.
- Recognize `make-hashtable` as R6RS.
- Reach for SRFI-125 `hash-table`.
- Probe:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125 hash-table: the replacement for R6RS make-hashtable.
(hash-table-size (hash-table))
```

Both probes confirm the analog is real and runs. The standards-awareness made the correction fast; the probe made it certain.

## The boundary in question-and-answer form

The standards boundary becomes clearest when practiced as a quick-answer drill. Read each question and answer it before reading the answer below.

**Q: Someone writes `(fold-left string-append \"\" strs)`. What's wrong?**
A: `fold-left` is R6RS, not Motoi. The correction is SRFI-1 `fold`, with the element (a string to prepend) arriving first. Since string concatenation builds left-to-right, `fold-right` may be more natural:

```scheme
;; fold-right to concatenate strings in order (element arrives before accumulator).
(fold-right string-append \"\" (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\"))
```

**Q: Someone writes `(make-hashtable string-hash string=?)`. What's wrong?**
A: `make-hashtable` is R6RS. Motoi's SRFI-125 hash table takes no hash function argument:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125: (hash-table) — no hash function argument. string keys work automatically.
(let ((h (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! h \"key\" \"value\")
  h)
```

**Q: Someone writes `(for-all number? (list 1 2 3))`. What's wrong?**
A: `for-all` is R6RS. The SRFI-1 name is `every`:

```scheme
;; every: SRFI-1's for-all. Returns #t if all pass.
(every number? (list 1 2 3))
```

**Q: A file begins with `(import (srfi :1))`. What's wrong?**
A: That's R7RS module ceremony for loading SRFI-1. In Motoi, SRFI-1 is already loaded. Delete the line:

```scheme
;; No import needed. SRFI-1 is here. fold runs immediately.
(fold + 0 (iota 5 1))
```

Four questions, four answers, four runnable confirmations. Each answer is the same structure: name the standard the stray came from, reach for the Motoi analog, prove it runs.

Motoi sits where it sits on purpose: R7RS-small semantics, four chosen SRFIs, no module ceremony, one fold, one hash-table spelling, SRFI-1 list predicates. Everything else in the Scheme family is a neighbor whose good ideas Motoi either adopted under a specific name or declined for the sake of clarity. When a Scheme-shaped verb fails, do not be surprised and do not be embarrassed — place it on this map, name its standard, reach for the analog, and run.

## The SRFIs not adopted: a placement guide

Beyond the four adopted SRFIs, many other numbered SRFIs carry real, running verbs — just not in Motoi. Knowing which SRFIs are absent lets you place strays correctly when they arrive.

**SRFI-8** (`receive`) — a syntax for capturing multiple values from a thunk, shorthand for `call-with-values`. Its form is `(receive formals expression body)`. Motoi uses R7RS-small's `call-with-values` and `let-values` for multiple values. `receive` is not wired:

```scheme
;; Motoi's multiple-value idiom: call-with-values (R7RS-small). Not SRFI-8 receive.
(call-with-values
  (lambda () (values 10 20 30))
  (lambda (a b c) (list a b c (+ a b c))))
```

**SRFI-43** (vectors) — extends the vector vocabulary with `vector-map`, `vector-for-each`, `vector-fold`, `vector-index`. These are real SRFI-43 verbs; Motoi uses the R7RS-small vector primitives instead. If you encounter `vector-map`, it is a SRFI-43 name and is not wired; the equivalent in Motoi is `vector->list`, then `map`, then `list->vector`:

```scheme
;; No SRFI-43 vector-map. The R7RS-small path: convert, map, convert back.
(list->vector
  (map (lambda (x) (* x x))
       (vector->list (vector 1 2 3 4 5))))
```

**SRFI-69** (hash tables) — an older hash-table SRFI whose names partially overlap with SRFI-125 and partially differ. SRFI-69 uses `make-hash-table` (with a hyphen but with an optional `equal?` argument), `hash-table-ref` without a `/default` suffix, and `hash-table-walk` for iteration. Motoi adopts SRFI-125, not SRFI-69. If you see `hash-table-walk`, it is SRFI-69; the SRFI-125 analog is `hash-table->alist` + `for-each`:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125 instead of SRFI-69 walk: alist + for-each.
(let ((h (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! h \"a\" 1)
  (hash-table-set! h \"b\" 2)
  (for-each (lambda (pair)
              (> (cdr pair) 0))   ; body substitute
            (hash-table->alist h)))
```

**SRFI-26** (`cut` / `cute`) — provides partial application syntax: `(cut + <> 5)` is a procedure that adds 5 to its argument. Not adopted. The Motoi equivalent is an explicit lambda:

```scheme
;; SRFI-26 (cut + <> 5) -> (lambda (x) (+ x 5)). Lambda is the real verb.
(map (lambda (x) (+ x 5)) (list 1 2 3 4))
```

**SRFI-11** (`let-values`) — R7RS-small actually incorporates `let-values` directly, so this one *is* present in Motoi. It is worth confirming because learners sometimes assume `let-values` is a SRFI-11 import:

```scheme
;; let-values is R7RS-small — not a SRFI you import. It just works.
(let-values (((a b) (values 10 20)))
  (+ a b))
```

The classification to hold: when a Scheme verb sounds like it might be a SRFI, ask first whether that SRFI is one of the four Motoi adopts. If not, the verb is a stray, but it is a polite one — a real numbered standard, just not yours.

## The `exact` / `inexact` name collision in detail

The exact-inexact naming difference between R6RS and R7RS-small is worth a deeper look because it is one of the few cases where *both names are perfectly correct Scheme* — just from different standards — and the failure mode is not a crash but an unbound error that looks puzzling.

R6RS renamed the conversion procedures: what R7RS-small calls `exact->inexact` became simply `inexact` in R6RS, and `inexact->exact` became `exact`. The logic was to reduce verbosity. The problem is that in Motoi (R7RS-small authority), the bare names `exact` and `inexact` as verbs do not bind:

```scheme
;; R7RS-small: exact->inexact and inexact->exact are the real names.
(list (exact->inexact 3/7)
      (inexact->exact 0.75))
```

When you see code using `(inexact x)` or `(exact x)` as a conversion call, you are looking at R6RS code. The correction is always the longer R7RS-small name. Some implementations do provide both for compatibility, but Motoi does not make that promise — the reference is R7RS-small, and the R7RS-small names are the ones to reach for.

## Going deeper on the fold argument order

The fold argument order shows up in contexts beyond simple summation, and each new context is worth working through to make the convention feel natural rather than memorized.

**Building a reversed list:** `(fold cons '() xs)` builds the reverse because each new element is `cons`ed onto the front of the accumulator. Element first, accumulator second — `cons` sees `(new-element, growing-reversed-list)`, and prepends. This is not a quirk; it is a consequence of the element-first convention:

```scheme
;; fold cons reverses because element-first => cons prepends each element.
(fold cons '() (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\" \"d\"))
```

**Building a running product:** `fold *` works exactly the same way as `fold +`. The multiplication is commutative, so order doesn't show, but the structure is:

```scheme
;; fold * (product): same element-first shape as fold +.
(fold * 1 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

**Accumulating into a hash table:** `fold` over a list of pairs can populate a hash table, with the accumulator being the table itself:

```scheme
;; fold to populate a hash table: element is the pair, accumulator is the table.
(fold (lambda (pair tbl)
        (hash-table-set! tbl (car pair) (cadr pair))
        tbl)
      (hash-table)
      (list (list \"x\" 10) (list \"y\" 20) (list \"z\" 30)))
```

That last fold is idiomatic and shows why element-first matters: the pair arrives before the table, which is the natural order for reading the lambda — \"given this pair and this table, set and return the table.\"

**The fold-right contrast:** where `fold` builds in reverse (left-to-right traversal, right-to-left construction with `cons`), `fold-right` builds in original order because it processes the list right-to-left. If order matters, the choice between `fold` and `fold-right` is exactly this distinction:

```scheme
;; fold-right string-append: processes right-to-left, so appends in original order.
(fold-right string-append \"\" (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\"))
```

```scheme
;; fold string-append: processes left-to-right, same result for string-append (commutative).
(fold string-append \"\" (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\"))
```

For `string-append`, order matters less because concatenation of the same strings in either direction gives the same result — but for operations like `cons` where order is everything, the fold direction determines the output.

## An extended Q&A drill on the standards boundary

These question-and-answer pairs are the kind that arise in real sessions. Work through each before reading the answer.

**Q: Code uses `(hashtable-set! h k v)`. What standard is that from?**
A: R6RS — no hyphen between `hash` and `table`. The SRFI-125 analog is `(hash-table-set! h k v)`. The only difference is the hyphen, but the hyphen is the whole boundary marker:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125: hyphenated hash-table-set!. The correction for hashtable-set!.
(let ((h (hash-table))) (hash-table-set! h \"k\" 1) (hash-table-ref/default h \"k\" 0))
```

**Q: Code uses `(for-each* f xs ys)` as a two-list for-each. Is that mine?**
A: No — `for-each*` under that exact name is not a standard SRFI or R7RS verb. The standard `for-each` is multi-list: `(for-each f xs ys)` applies `f` to parallel elements from `xs` and `ys`. No asterisk needed:

```scheme
;; for-each is already multi-list in R7RS-small. No for-each* needed.
(for-each (lambda (x y) (+ x y))
          (list 1 2 3)
          (list 10 20 30))
```

**Q: Code uses `(string-for-each f s)`. Is that R7RS-small?**
A: Yes — `string-for-each` is R7RS-small core. It applies a procedure to each character of a string for side effects:

```scheme
;; string-for-each: R7RS-small. Applies proc to each character.
(let ((chars '()))
  (string-for-each (lambda (c) (set! chars (cons c chars))) \"abc\")
  (reverse chars))
```

**Q: A SRFI-1 list processing library defines `span`. Is it mine?**
A: SRFI-1 defines `span` and `break`, but not every Motoi build wires the full SRFI-1 shelf. The safe substitute is `take-while` (confirmed present) for the first half, and `drop-while` for the second:

```scheme
;; take-while + drop-while: the confirmed span equivalent in Motoi.
(let ((xs (list 2 4 6 1 3 5)))
  (list (take-while even? xs)
        (drop-while even? xs)))
```

**Q: Someone writes `(list-head xs n)`. Is that mine?**
A: No — `list-head` is not a standard R7RS-small or SRFI-1 name. The real verb for taking the first `n` elements is `take`:

```scheme
;; list-head is not mine. take is. Same operation, SRFI-1 name.
(take (list 10 20 30 40 50) 3)
```

Five Q&A pairs, five placement facts. The standards map makes each one answerable in seconds: name the standard, find the analog, run the analog.

## Why the standards boundary is the deepest edge

The boundary from Chapter 1 has three regions, and region two — excluded standards — is the one that takes the most deliberate learning because the strays there are *correct Scheme*, written down in published documents, running in other implementations. They fail in Motoi not because they are wrong but because Motoi made a different choice. That specific failure — a real standard verb, wrong dialect — is the one that trips up the most careful programmers and produces the most puzzling errors.

The fold argument order, the hash-table spelling, the module ceremony, the list-predicate names: these are the five core cases, and each one can be learned cold in a morning. The standard that produced each stray, the Motoi analog, and the fact that distinguishes them — element-first vs accumulator-first, hyphen vs no-hyphen, `every` vs `for-all` — are a small vocabulary that pays off continuously. Once you hold those five distinctions, the standards boundary becomes the easiest part of the Motoi edge to navigate, because every stray from it has an immediate, clean, runnable answer.

The next chapter turns from the standards next door to the *languages* next door, and to the plain, kind sentence Motoi says to each of them: I'm not that language. I'm Motoi.")
